 Côte d'Ivoire.svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P41","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q1008","kind":"coat","value_qid":null,"value_label":"Coat of arms of Ivory Coast (2).svg","from_t":null,"to_t":null,"title":null,"property":"P94","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":null},{"qid":"Q1008","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q179242","value_label":"Laurent Gbagbo","from_t":2000.8169398907103,"to_t":2011.2739726027398,"title":"President of the Ivory Coast","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Laurent Gbagbo"},{"qid":"Q1008","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q214407","value_label":"Félix Houphouët-Boigny","from_t":1960.8387978142077,"to_t":1993.9315068493152,"title":"President of the Ivory Coast","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Félix Houphouët-Boigny"},{"qid":"Q1008","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q357779","value_label":"Henri Konan Bédié","from_t":1993.9315068493152,"to_t":1999.978082191781,"title":"President of the Ivory Coast","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Henri Konan Bédié"},{"qid":"Q1008","kind":"ruler","value_qid":"Q357802","value_label":"Robert Guéï","from_t":1999.978082191781,"to_t":2000.8169398907103,"title":"President of the Ivory Coast","property":"P39","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"Robert Guéï"},{"qid":"Q1008","kind":"flag","value_qid":"Q102967","value_label":"Flag of Côte d'Ivoire.svg","from_t":1959.9205479452055,"to_t":null,"title":"flag of Ivory Coast","property":"P163","retrieved":"2026-10-30","vl":"flag of Ivory Coast"}],"people":{"Q57413":{"label":"Alassane Ouattara","description":"President of Ivory Coast since 2010","enwiki":"Alassane Ouattara","summary":"Alassane Dramane Ouattara (; French pronunciation: [alasan wataʁa]; born 1 January 1942) is an Ivorian politician and economist who has been President of Ivory Coast (Côte d'Ivoire) since 2010. An economist by profession, he worked for the International Monetary Fund (IMF) and the Central Bank of West African States (French: Banque Centrale des Etats de l'Afrique de l'Ouest, BCEAO), and was the Prime Minister of Côte d'Ivoire from November 1990 to December 1993, appointed to that post by then-President Félix Houphouët-Boigny. Ouattara became the president of the Rally of the Republicans (RDR), an Ivorian political party, in 1999.","image":"Alassane Ouattara - 2024 (cropped).jpg","birth":1942,"death":null},"Q3434569":{"label":"Robert Beugré Mambé","description":"Ivorian politician","enwiki":"Robert Beugré Mambé","summary":"Robert Beugré Mambé is an Ivorian civil engineer and politician who has been the Prime Minister of Ivory Coast since 2023. Prior to his tenure as prime minister he was the governor of the Autonomous District of Abidjan and president of the Independent Electoral Commission.","image":"Robert Beugré Mambé 2.jpg","birth":1952,"death":null},"Q179242":{"label":"Laurent Gbagbo","description":"4th President of Ivory Coast","enwiki":"Laurent Gbagbo","summary":"Koudou Laurent Gbagbo (born 31 May 1945) is an Ivorian politician who served as the president of Côte d'Ivoire from 2000 until his arrest in April 2011. He was the first president in the history of the country that was a centre-left politician. A historian, Gbagbo was imprisoned in the early 1970s and again in the early 1990s, and he lived in exile in France during much of the 1980s as a result of his union activism. Gbagbo founded the Ivorian Popular Front (FPI) in 1982 and ran unsuccessfully for president against Félix Houphouët-Boigny at the start of multi-party politics in 1990.","image":"Laurent Gbagbo (2008).jpg","birth":1945.4109589041095,"death":null},"Q214407":{"label":"Félix Houphouët-Boigny","description":"First President of Ivory Coast, from 1960 to 1993 (1905-1993)","enwiki":"Félix Houphouët-Boigny","summary":"Félix Houphouët-Boigny (French: [feliks ufwɛ(t) bwaɲi]; 18 October 1905 – 7 December 1993), affectionately called Papa Houphouët or Le Vieux (\"the old one\"), was an Ivorian politician and physician who served as the first president of Ivory Coast, from 1960 until his death in 1993. A tribal chief, he worked as a medical aide, union leader, and planter before being elected to the French Parliament in 1945. He served in several ministerial positions within the Government of France before leading Ivory Coast following independence in 1960. Throughout his life, he played a significant role in politics and the decolonisation of Africa.","image":"Félix Houphouët-Boigny 1962-07-16.jpg","birth":1905.7945205479452,"death":1993.9315068493152},"Q357779":{"label":"Henri Konan Bédié","description":"President of Ivory Coast from 1993 to 1999 (1934-2023)","enwiki":"Henri Konan Bédié","summary":"Aimé Henri Konan Bédié (5 May 1934 – 1 August 2023) was an Ivorian politician. He was President of Côte d'Ivoire from 1993 to 1999, and formerly President of the Democratic Party of Ivory Coast – African Democratic Rally (PDCI–RDA). Prior to becoming president, he was a member and president of the National Assembly of Ivory Coast. He unsuccessfully sought another term as president in the 2020 presidential election.","image":"Henri Konan Bédié (P001954018AH).jpg","birth":1934.3397260273973,"death":2023.5808219178082},"Q357802":{"label":"Robert Guéï","description":"Ivorian politician (1941-2002)","enwiki":"Robert Guéï","summary":"Robert Guéï (French pronunciation: [ʁɔbɛʁ ɡe.i]; 16 March 1941 – 19 September 2002) was an Ivorian politician who served as the third president of the Ivory Coast from 24 December 1999 to 26 October 2000. He succeeded President Henri Konan Bédié after the 1999 Ivorian coup d'état and lost to Laurent Gbagbo in the ensuing 2000 Ivorian presidential election. Guéï, his wife Rose Doudou Guéï, and his children were killed on 19 September 2002 on the first day of the First Ivorian Civil War.","image":"Robert Guéï.jpg","birth":1941.2027397260274,"death":2002.7150684931507}},"media":{"Flag of Côte d'Ivoire.svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Flag_of_C%C3%B4te_d%27Ivoire.svg/330px-Flag_of_C%C3%B4te_d%27Ivoire.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_C%C3%B4te_d%27Ivoire.svg","author":"See File history below for details.","license":"Public domain"},"Coat of arms of Ivory Coast (2).svg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Coat_of_arms_of_Ivory_Coast_%282%29.svg/330px-Coat_of_arms_of_Ivory_Coast_%282%29.svg.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Ivory_Coast_(2).svg","author":"Republic of Côte d'Ivoirevectored byFOX 52","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},"Alassane Ouattara - 2024 (cropped).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Alassane_Ouattara_-_2024_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Alassane_Ouattara_-_2024_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alassane_Ouattara_-_2024_(cropped).jpg","author":"Dario Pignatelli / European Union","license":"Attribution"},"Robert Beugré Mambé 2.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/79/Robert_Beugr%C3%A9_Mamb%C3%A9_2.jpg/330px-Robert_Beugr%C3%A9_Mamb%C3%A9_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Robert_Beugr%C3%A9_Mamb%C3%A9_2.jpg","author":"Prisca diom","license":"CC BY 4.0"},"Laurent Gbagbo (2008).jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Laurent_Gbagbo_%282008%29.jpg/330px-Laurent_Gbagbo_%282008%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Laurent_Gbagbo_(2008).jpg","author":"Zenman","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},"Félix Houphouët-Boigny 1962-07-16.jpg":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/common